A||N||19||Bluebottlejunky||Lowring the Fiero||||10||
Z||000000||Bluebottlejunky||01-31-2001||02:59 PM||||Hey,<BR>   I have a question about lowring the Fiero.  I know that you can buy lowring springs....what about cutting springs? How is it done? is it good quality?  Is it safe?<P>Thanks <BR>Mike Got Fiero? <P>(youngest person on Fiero Forum! Sorry Alex) lol||||reg||10
Z||000001||Wade87GT||01-31-2001||04:06 PM||||There are lots of ways to lower the Fiero, some good and some are pretty bad. Try using the search feature in the upper right of the page. Then expect to be here awhile reading all of the info.||||reg||14
Z||000002||Smokin87||01-31-2001||04:34 PM||||DONT CUT THE SPRINGS?<BR>if you cut the springs you could seriously dammage the car while driving.  The difference between lowering springs and normal springs is the distance between each turn.  unless you have the right amount of turns, and stiffness to the springs, you could bottom out real easily, and damage the undercarrage, and ground effects of the car.  as well as some other vital compents such as the exhaust.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_copy.jpg">  <BR>"When man wanted to make the car faster...he invented the jet-plane."||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||01-31-2001||08:29 PM||||As Wade87GT suggests, check past posts.<P>Cutting the springs <B>can</B> work, if you don't do it to excess.  (Yes, I know -- restraint is no fun.)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||01-31-2001||08:55 PM||||I have never liked the thought of cutting springs to lower a car, but that has now changed. I ordered the Fiero Store's springs for my 88 GT and after installing them, the rear looked great- exactly 1 inch lower than stock. The front just kept on getting lower... and lower and lower. After a day, it was on the rubber bump stops, so I trimmed them down. A couple of days later, it is on the stops again. The springs will just not hold the car up.<BR>So, I cut one complete coil from the original front springs and reinstalled them. Well, the car sits pretty even front to back and the ride is firm but not unbearable. It is much better than when I had the Fiero store springs installed. On the front, one coil was one inch, but the back may be completely different- just DO NOT use a torch to cut them. A cut off wheel works great.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||01-31-2001||09:01 PM||||i know that jcwhitney sells lowering springs for the fiero, last time i checked. i think a set for the rear is $80. maybe the same price for the front. lowers it 1 or 1 1/2 inches i believe.<P><BR>(woohoo! 500 posts!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 01-31-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||DreXteR||02-01-2001||12:04 AM||||There is a topic in the general chat about a set of intrax springs for only 170 or so. Check there, I will recive a set from him. <P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric||02-01-2001||01:30 AM||||How does your car handle now, Cooter?  Did you get rid of that transition wobble?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||02-01-2001||09:01 AM||||The car is stuck like glue! When I replaced the front springs, I found one of the shocks was bad (stuck in a partial extended position). After getting that fixed, the problems appears to be gone.||||reg||1
Z||000009||danny boy||02-01-2001||05:55 PM|||| I got a set of springs from the Fiero Store. Oh my gosh what pieces of S they are!!! The 1" drop turned out to be 3". I have coilovers on the back.I got them from Held motorsports and aside from my grant corsa steering wheel they are the best thing I have ever bought for any of my cars.<BR> You can get any spring rate you want, adjust ride height, and it makes it real easy to corner weight the car.||||reg||1
Z||000010||gixxer||02-01-2001||07:03 PM||||Sent this letter to The Fiero Store today:<BR>------------<BR>Awhile back, I contacted you about the spring rates for your in house sport springs for the 1988 Fiero, and last week I ordered up a full set.<BR>However, lately I have seen a few people express some dissatisfaction regarding these springs. The problems seems to be of a quality control nature.<BR>Here is ONE of the threads: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008187.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008187.html</A> <BR>This Fiero site is probably the largest and most active one in the world, and your springs are getting beat up pretty badly, as is your good name.<BR>Now I am getting somewhat nervous as to the wisdom of my purchase. <BR>Since manufacturing springs is a science rather than an art, what steps have you since taken to ensure that I will not have the same complaint as these other people?<BR>And if I do, what steps do I take to return them?<BR>Thanks,<BR>Brian<BR>------------<BR>What do you suppose I'll get back?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Voytek||02-02-2001||02:47 PM||||Hey everyone<BR>I've seen a few topics on lowering a Fiero through various methods.  I've seen (and been in) a Fiero with cut front springs and a new set of Eibachs on the rear.  It seemed to handle really well.  Seeing all the concern about the Fiero Store's springs, I'm considering cutting the rear ones.  Can this be done?  I mean, the rear coils are spaced pretty far apart.  Would the springs not bulge outward after re-installation?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||02-02-2001||04:09 PM||||I have FS lowering springs in my 88 Formula for about 2 years now. Very happy with them. Have not had the problems mentioned here. Maybe they changed supplier. Look my car here;<BR> <A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/alexft/Alex4mula/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://community-2.webtv.net/alexft/Alex4mula/index.html</A> <P>Previously I had cut the fronts one coil. Drop was nice but ride was a little harsher than with the lowering ones.||||reg||1
Z||000013||gixxer||02-02-2001||06:41 PM||||The Fiero Stores Reply to my E-mail..<BR>--------------------<BR>Thank you for your quick response, Justin.<BR>I just received the new springs earlier today.<BR>After measuring them, I will touch up the paint and install them.<BR>I will post your message on Pennocks Fiero forum.<BR>Thanks again.<BR> <BR>----- Original Message ----- <BR>From: The Fiero Store <BR>To: Brian O'Toole <BR>Sent: Friday, February 02, 2001 7:52 AM<BR>Subject: Re: 1988 Fiero Lowering Springs<P><BR>Brian,<BR> <BR>Lowering springs have always been a tricky product to sell for all Fieros since lowering distance and expected ride quality are matters of personal preference. Of the few complaints that we have received (on 88 springs as well as the early Eibach springs), no specific pattern has evolved. Some have told us the springs lower the car too much, some not enough, some too stiff while others say they are not stiff enough. This makes it very difficult to request any changes from our manufacturer. Our only other alternative would be to discontinue the product line. Of the nearly 100 sets of 88 springs sold annually, less than 10% get returned. Therefore, we cannot justify discontinuing the product.<BR> <BR>Our spring manufacturer has the factory spring heights and rates. We tell them the approximate drop and spring rate we would like. We have sold the same 1988 springs from the same manufacturer since we opened and have had a much larger percentage of positive results than negative. <BR> <BR>We will continue trying to satisfy all of our customers, but unfortunately, it is very difficult to make everyone happy. There are very few steps we can take to completely eliminate complaints about any of the products we sell. We strive to market all of our products to the best of our abilities and if a product fails to meet market standards, it is discontinued immediately. <BR> <BR>As our catalog states, returns must be received within 60 days. Therefore, if you are not completely satisfied with the springs, please e-mail or call for a return authorization number and we will be more than willing to take them back. Please keep in mind that your factory bump stops should be removed since your suspension travel will be decreased.<BR> <BR>Please let me know if you have any other questions or concerns.<BR>Thank you,<BR>Justin<BR>---------------------------<BR>Having any bare metal exposed on a springs working coils will compromise long term durability.||||reg||1
Z||000014||G-Nasty||02-03-2001||12:03 AM||||I removed my bel-tec spindles from one of my GT's to my better one. The older cars front end was 2 high. Grind wheel works great-height was perfect and that car doesn't bottom out. Had to rethread spindle bolts etc. but removal & installation was E-Z. You will have to get adjustable ball joints because the negative camber IS noticeable without using drop spindles. Also car may look toe-out so get alignment.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrossum||02-03-2001||12:02 PM||||i used a chop saw and cut my fronts and rears. lowered the car about 2 1/2 inches all around. the ride is quite a bit stiffer, but i autocross the car, so stiffer is better. just my penny's worth.||||reg||10
Z||000016||ginoferrari||08-02-2001||01:10 AM||||mrossum<BR>    How much did you cut off of your springs? What other changes have you made to your car for autocrossing? I'm going to start doing some autocrossing and could use any help you can give for mods to improve handleing, and performance.<p>[This message has been edited by ginoferrari (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000017||ichifiero||08-02-2001||08:12 PM||||I have seen some info on lowering spindles. How does this compare to lowering springs?||||reg||1
Z||000018||starfighter007@msn.com||08-02-2001||09:34 PM||||Lowering spindles are the best way to go.But,this some what very expensive.I dont remember exactly I think the spindles are in the area of $230.00 each.I think that is way out of line for what they try to get for them.I realize they need to recover the cost of makeing them.I am sure if they cut the cost in half they would recover there cost much quicker then selling a few at a time I would definetly buy a set if thet were priced realalisticly||||reg||6
Z||000019||Tigger||08-02-2001||11:32 PM||||ichifiero, be sure to look into <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/~strdream/" TARGET=_blank>Street Dreams</A> spindles if you haven't already.<P>The advantage drop spindles have over drop springs is that they maintain the stock suspension geometry and ride travels.<P>If you intend on keeping your Fiero for a long time and want to put a lot of miles on it, go with drop spindles.  I have a set on my Fiero and I've been very pleased.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||1
